Call sites re-derived, not trusted. grep -rn "load_project_context" --include=*.rs . → the direct loader call in commands_spawn.rs exists only inside spawn_project_context() (488–512); the two spawn paths read it through that seam at 857 and 1009. So the issue's count of two is right and its line numbers (:810, :961) are stale — the re-derivation the task demanded was worth doing.

The real gap, and what I landed. Neither existing test could fail on this — the pure pair never touches the global, and a_restricted_parent_confines_its_worker_through_the_real_global writes it (set_safe_mode(true)) instead of driving the flag into it. The unpinned edge was parse_args("--safe-mode"/"--restricted") → Config.safe_mode → main.rs::apply_config_flags → global → seam. New test a_safe_mode_flag_reaches_the_worker_seam_through_config_and_the_real_global closes it, with --restricted in the loop because it reaches the global without naming safe mode in argv.

Positive control, run serially as one atomic mutate→run→restore: deleting the set_safe_mode(true) copy in apply_config_flags reddens exactly the new test; the eight pre-existing safe_mode-matching tests stay green — the direction that proves the older three could not have caught it. Restored, and re-measured the CLI arms afterward (1/0/0 unchanged).

Stated limit: pins the parent's decision reaching the worker seam, one indirection short of the worker's prompt (that needs a live model turn). In a fork with no instruction file the loader returns None anyway — hence the anti-vacuous presence assertion.

